<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<section xml:id="getting-started-introduction-first-look-at-the-editor">
  <title>Godot: First look at Godot's interface</title>
  <introduction>
  <p>This page will give you a brief overview of Godot's interface. We're going to
            look at the different main screens and docks to help you situate yourself.</p>
    <note>
      <title>See Also</title>
      <p>For a comprehensive breakdown of the editor's interface and how to
                use it, see the <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/editor/index.html#doc-editor-introduction">Editor manual</url>.</p>
      </note>
    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-introduction-first-look-at-the-editor-the-project-manager">
      <title>The Project Manager</title>
      <p>When you launch Godot, the first window you see is the Project Manager. In the
                default tab <term>Projects</term>, you can manage existing projects, import or create new
                ones, and more.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Projects Tab</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_project_manager.webp"  width="75%">
         <shortdescription>The Godot Project Manager window is shown with no projects in the projects tab and the search bar selected.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>At the top of the window, there is another tab named <term>Asset Library</term>. The first
                time you go to this tab you'll see a "Go Online" button. For privacy reasons, the Godot
                project manager does not access the internet by default. To change this click
                the "Go Online" button. You can change this option later in the settings.</p>
      <p>Once your network mode is set to "online", you can search for demo projects in the open
                source asset library, which includes many projects developed by the community:</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Asset Library Tab</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_project_templates.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>The Godot Project Manager window is shown with 8 assets showing in the Asset Library tab and the search bar selected.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>The Project Manager's settings can be opened using the <term>Settings</term> menu:</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Settings Menu</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_settings.webp" >
         <shortdescription>The Godot Project Manager window is shown with the settings menu button in the upper right hand corner in a yellow box.</shortdescription>
         <description><p>The Godot Project Manager window is shown with no projects in the projects tab and the search bar selected. The settings menu button in the upper right hand corner is shown in a yellow box.</p></description>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>From here, you can change the editor's language (default is the system language), interface theme, display
                scale, network mode, and also the directory naming convention.</p>
      <note>
        <title>See Also</title>
        <p>To learn the Project Manager's ins and outs, read
                    <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/editor/project_manager.html#doc-project-manager">Using the Project Manager</url>.</p>
        </note>
      </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-introduction-first-look-at-the-editor-first-look-at-godot-s-editor">
      <title>First look at Godot's editor</title>
      <p>When you open a new or an existing project, the editor's interface appears.
                Let's look at its main areas:</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Default Editor View</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_editor_empty.webp" width="75%">
         <shortdescription>Godot's Editor view is shown with the 3D tab selected in the middle. An empty project is shown.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>By default, along the window's top edge, it features <term>main menu</term> on the left, <term>workspace</term> switching
                buttons in the center (active workspace is highlighted), and <term>playtest</term> buttons and the
                <term>Movie Maker Mode</term> toggle on the right:</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Editor Top Edge</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_top_menus.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>The top edge of the editor window is shown with
            the 3D Tab selected. 
         </shortdescription>
         <description><p>The top edge of the editor window is shown with menus Scene, Project, Debug, Editor, and Help on the left side.
            The middle has tabs for 2D, 3D, Script, Game, and AssetLib. 
            The 3D Tab is selected. On the right are action buttons for
            run project, pause project, stop project, remote deploy, run current scene,
            run specific scene, and enable movie maker mode.</p>
         </description>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Just below the workspace buttons, the opened <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.html#doc-key-concepts-overview-scenes">scenes</url>
                as tabs are seen. The plus (+) button right next to the tabs will add a new scene to the project.
                With the button on the far right, distraction-free mode can be toggled, which maximizes or restores
                the <term>viewport</term>'s size by hiding <term>docks</term> in the interface:</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Open Scenes Tabs</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_scene_selector.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>The [empty] scene tab and a button to add a new scene are shown in the bar.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>In the center, below the scene selector is the <term>viewport</term> with its <term>toolbar</term> at the top, where you'll
                find different tools to move, scale, or lock the scene's nodes (currently the 3D workspace is active):</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Scene 3D Viewport</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_3d_viewport.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>An empty 3D viewport is shown with it's toolbar in select mode directly above.
         </shortdescription>
         <description><p>An empty 3D viewport is shown with it's toolbar directly above.
            Select mode is selected in the toolbar. The toolbar has the buttones
            Select mode, move mode, rotate mode, scale mode, show list of nodes, etc.
         </p>
        </description>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>This toolbar changes based on the context and selected node. Here is the 2D toolbar:</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>2D Toolbar</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_toolbar_2d.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>A 2D toolbar is shown with
            Select mode active in the toolbar.
        </shortdescription>
        <description><p>A 2D toolbar is shown.
            Select mode is selected in the toolbar. The toolbar has the buttones
            Select mode, move mode, rotate mode, scale mode, show list of nodes, etc.
        </p></description>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Below is the 3D one:</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>3D Toolbar</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_toolbar_3d.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>A 3D toolbar is shown with
            Select mode selected in the toolbar. 
        </shortdescription>
        <description><p>A 3D toolbar is shown.
            Select mode is selected in the toolbar. The toolbar has the buttones
            Select mode, move mode, rotate mode, scale mode, show list of nodes, etc.
        </p></description>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <note>
        <title>See Also</title>
        <p><ul><li>To learn more on workspaces, read <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/getting_started/introduction/first_look_at_the_editor.html#the-five-main-screens">The five main screens</url>.</li>
            <li>To learn more on the 3D viewport and 3D in general, read <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/3d/introduction_to_3d.html#doc-introduction-to-3d">Introduction to 3D</url>.</li>
        </ul>
    </p>
        </note>
      <p>On either side of the viewport sit the <term>docks</term>. And at the bottom of the
                window lies the <term>bottom panel</term>.</p>
      <p>Let's look at the docks. The <term>FileSystem</term> dock lists your project files, including
                scripts, images, audio samples, and more:</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Filesystem Dock</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_filesystem_dock.webp"  width="25%" >
         <shortdescription>A view of the filesystem dock shows that res:// is selected.
         </shortdescription>
         <description><p>A view of the filesystem dock shows that res:// is selected
            in the file selection text box. Beneath that, there is a filter files text box.
            And below that is the file listing that shows one file called icon.svg in the root
            res:// directory.
         </p>
         </description>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>The <term>Scene</term> dock lists the active scene's nodes:</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Scene Dock</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_scene_dock.webp"  width="25%" >
         <shortdescription>The scene dock is shown without any nodes created yet.
         </shortdescription>
         <description><p>The scene dock is shown with an add Node button, a link button, a filter box and a button for additional selections.
            There are four default node creation buttons,
            2D Scene, 3D Scene, User Interface, and Other Node.
         </p>
         </description>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>The <term>Inspector</term> allows you to edit the properties of a selected node:</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Inspector Dock</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_inspector_dock.webp"  width="25%">
         <shortdescription>The Inspector dock is shown for a Node 2D. 
         </shortdescription>
         <description><p>The Inspector dock is shown for a Node 2D. 
            There is a filter properties text box followed by
            selections for transform, visibility, ordering, etc.
         </p>
         </description>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <note>
        <title>See Also</title>
        <p>To read more on inspector, see <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/editor/inspector_dock.html#doc-editor-inspector-dock">Inspector Dock</url>.</p>
         <p>Docks can be customized. Read more on <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/editor/customizing_editor.html#doc-customizing-editor-moving-docks">Moving and resizing docks</url>.</p>
        </note>
      <p>The <term>bottom panel</term>, situated below the viewport, is the host for the debug
                console, the animation editor, the audio mixer, and more. They can take precious
                space, that's why they're folded by default:</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Bottom Panels Tabs</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_bottom_panels.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>The folded bottom panel is shown with available tabs.
         </shortdescription>
         <description><p>The folded bottom panel is shown with available tabs Output,
            Debugger, Audio, Animation, and Shader Editor.
         </p>
        </description>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>When you click on one, it expands vertically. Below, you can see the animation editor opened:</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Animation Panel (Bottom)</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_bottom_panel_animation.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>
            The expanded Animation Panel is shown with no animations.
         </shortdescription>
         <description><p>The expanded Animation Panel is shown with no animations.
            There are buttons to control the animations a the top, and
            buttons to change some animation settings at the bottom.
         </p>
        </description>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Bottom panels can also be shown or hidden using the shortcuts defined in
                <term>Editor Settings &gt; Shortcuts</term>, under the <term>Bottom Panels</term> category.</p>
      </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-introduction-first-look-at-the-editor-the-five-main-screens">
      <title>The five main screens</title>
      <p>There are five main screen buttons centered at the top of the editor:
                2D, 3D, Script, Game and Asset Library.</p>
      <p>You'll use the <term>2D screen</term> for all types of games. In addition to 2D games,
                the 2D screen is where you'll build your interfaces.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Example 2D Workspace</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_workspace_2d.webp" width="75%">
         <shortdescription>
            An example of a 2D platformer game is shown.
         </shortdescription>
         <description><p>An example of a 2D game is shown with 
            a scene that has more Nodes than you can see. It looks like a one
            player platformer.
         </p>
         </description>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>In the <term>3D screen</term>, you can work with meshes, lights, and design levels for
                3D games.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Example 3D Workspace</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_workspace_3d.webp" width="75%">
         <shortdescription>An example of a 3D game with a glowing orange 
            orb in the center held by metal arms.
         </shortdescription>
         <description><p>An example of a 3D game with a scene that has a glowing orange 
            orb in the center held by metal arms. There are many nodes and assets shown
            on the left side.
         </p>
         </description>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <note>
        <title>Note</title>
        <p>Read <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/3d/introduction_to_3d.html#doc-introduction-to-3d">Introduction to 3D</url> for more detail about the <term>3D
                    main screen</term>.</p>
        </note>
      <p>The <term>Game screen</term> is where your project will appear when running it from
                the editor. You can go through your project to test it, and pause it and
                adjust it in real time. Note that this is for testing how adjustments would
                work, any changes made here are not saved when the game stops running.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Game Screen</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_workspace_game.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>An example of the start screen of a Game called TPS Demo.
         </shortdescription>
         <description><p>An example of the start screen of a Game called TPS Demo in the Game Screen.
            There is a play, play online, settings, and quit button on the left.
            There is a 3D image of a robot on the right.
         </p>
         </description>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>The <term>Script screen</term> is a complete code editor with a debugger, rich
                auto-completion, and built-in code reference.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Script Screen</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_workspace_script.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>An image of an unsaved scene tab with a character_body_3d.gd script.
         </shortdescription>
         <description><p>An image of an unsaved scene tab, with a 
            script called character_body_3d.gd as the only open script.
            The first 21 lines of the script are shown, and a method called
            _physics_process is shown on the bottom left.
         </p>
         </description>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Finally, the <term>Asset Library</term> is a library of free and open source add-ons, scripts,
                and assets to use in your projects.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Asset Library</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_workspace_assetlib.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>The AssetLib tab is is shown with 6 assets showing.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <note>
        <title>See Also</title>
        <p>You can learn more about the asset library in
                    <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/community/asset_library/what_is_assetlib.html#doc-what-is-assetlib">About the Asset Library</url>.</p>
        </note>
      </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-introduction-first-look-at-the-editor-integrated-class-reference">
      <title>Integrated class reference</title>
      <p>Godot comes with a built-in class reference.</p>
      <p>You can search for information about a class, method, property, constant, or
                signal by any one of the following methods:</p>
      <p>
        <ul>
        <li>Pressing <c>F1</c> (or <c>Opt</c>
          <c />+<c />
          <c>Space</c> on macOS, or <c>Fn</c>
          <c />+<c />
          <c>F1</c> for laptops
                        with a <c>Fn</c> key) anywhere in the editor.</li>
        <li>Clicking the "Search Help" button in the top-right of the Script main screen.</li>
        <li>Clicking on the Help menu and Search Help.</li>
        <li>
          <c>Ctrl</c>
          <c />+<c />
          <c>Click</c> (<c>Cmd</c>
          <c />+<c />
          <c>Click</c> on macOS) on a class name, function name,
                        or built-in variable in the script editor.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Search Help Button</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_search_help_button.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>The Search Help button is shown
            in a yellow box in the Script Tab of the Godot Editor.
         </shortdescription>
         <description><p>The top portion of the Godot editor is shown with the 
            Script tab selected with both the help menu item and the Search Help button 
            in a yellow box.</p>
         </description>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>When you do any of these, a window pops up. Type to search for any item. You can
                also use it to browse available objects and methods.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>"animated" search</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_search_help.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>Search help results are shown for the search animated.
            the AnimatedSprite2D entry is selected.
         </shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Double-click on an item to open the corresponding page in the script main screen.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>AnimatedSprite2D help doc</caption>
        <image source="editor_intro_help_class_animated_sprite.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>The help documentation for the AnimatedScript2D
            is shown in the script tab of the godot editor.
         </shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Alternatively,</p>
      <p>
      <ul>
        <li>Clicking while pressing <c>Ctrl</c> (<c>Cmd</c> on macOS) on a class name, function name,
                        or built-in variable in the script editor.</li>
        <li>Right-clicking on nodes and choosing <term>Open Documentation</term> or choosing <term>Lookup Symbol</term>
                        for elements in script editor will directly open their documentation.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      </subsection>
    </section>
